 "where": {
  "title": "Where",
  "one": "A question word to learn in five easy minutes. The question mark floats above her head, asking.",
  "seoTitle": "“Where” Sight Word Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach “Where” (Class 29)",
  "metaDescription": "A Class 29 lesson on the sight word “Where”, a question word: read the card, play one quick game and spot it in a real story. Step by step for parents of 4–5 year olds.",
  "intro": "Five minutes, one card: you will play hide-and-seek and ask “where is it?” while you search, and your child will meet “where” in a real sentence, “Where is my shoe?” By the end, “where” should be a word they know at a glance.",
  "what": "“Where” is a question word. On this card, a girl with a question mark above her head looks around with her hands on her hips, with the word printed in big, clear letters underneath.",
  "mean": "“Where” opens a question and asks about a place, as in “Where is my shoe?”",
  "know": "Your child needs to recognise “where” at a glance, say it clearly, and begin to hear it in the stories you read together. Good hunting grounds: lost things, hide-and-seek and “where are we going?”.",
  "why": "Question words like “where” help a child ask and understand questions, and questions are how children and stories talk to each other.",
  "dev": "Asking questions, listening for answers and recognition of a high-frequency word.",
  "vocab": "word, read, question",
  "say": "Look at this card. It says ‘where’. Where is my shoe? Now you say ‘where’. Well done!",
  "teach": "1) Show the card and read “where” clearly, running your finger under the letters. Let your child say it back, twice.\n2) Look at the picture together: a girl with a question mark above her head looks around with her hands on her hips. Ask what is happening, then link the picture to the word.\n3) Play one tiny game: play hide-and-seek and ask “where is it?” while you search. Read the word one last time and stop while it is still fun.",
  "notice": "Point to the word first, then to the picture. Cover the picture with your hand and read the word; lift your hand to check. Then let your child trace the first letter with one finger.",
  "ask": "“What does the card say?” “What is she asking?” “Can you ask me a where question?” — answered with a read-aloud and a question of their own.",
  "childSay": "Expect your child to ask “where” questions all day, which is the best practice of all.",
  "real": "Watch for “where” here: lost things, hide-and-seek and “where are we going?”. Every sighting is a free practice round: point, say it, move on.",
  "play": "Play hide-and-seek and ask “where is it?” while you search. Finish by saying “where” one more time.",
  "check": "Tomorrow, hold up the card without saying a word. If your child reads “where” straight away, the word has arrived; if not, play the game once more and try again the day after.",
  "tips": "If “where” does not stick today, do not push. Slip the card into tomorrow’s pile and try again with a smile."
 },
